diff --git a/docs/adr/0004-redis-rate-limit-and-cache.md b/docs/adr/0004-redis-rate-limit-and-cache.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5dec2be
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/adr/0004-redis-rate-limit-and-cache.md
@@ -0,0 +1,26 @@
+# ADR 0004: Redis for rate limiting and caching, not for correctness
+
+**Status:** accepted
+
+## Context
+Two needs came up once the API could run as several replicas:
+1. **Abuse protection.** A compromised session or a buggy client can fire hundreds of transfers a minute from one account. Balance and per-transfer limits stop money from going negative, but not the flood itself.
+2. **Cheap reads.** `GET /transfers/{id}` is called on every retry and status poll, and each call hits PostgreSQL.
+
+An in-memory limiter or cache would be per-pod: with 2 replicas, a client gets 2x the limit and each pod has its own cache. The state has to be shared, so it lives in Redis.
+
+## Decision
+- **Rate limit:** fixed window per source account (`kirim:rl:transfers:{account}:{window}`), default 10 new transfers per minute. `INCR` + `EXPIRE` run in one Lua script, so a counter can never be left without a TTL. Over the limit: `429` with `Retry-After`.
+- The check runs **after** the idempotency key is reserved, so replays of an earlier request use no quota, and a `429` rolls back the reservation so the client can retry later with the same key.
+- **Cache:** cache-aside for transfers only, TTL 10 minutes. Transfers are immutable once committed, so there is nothing to invalidate. Balances are **not** cached: they change on every transfer and a stale balance in a bank is a bug.
+- Entries are written on the read path only, never inside the write transaction (which could still roll back).
+- **Redis is never the source of truth.** Idempotency stays in PostgreSQL (see ADR 0001: Redis can't be atomic with the PostgreSQL write).
+
+## Failure behaviour
+- Redis down → the limiter **fails open** by default (`kirim.rate-limit.fail-open`), cache reads fall through to PostgreSQL. Transfers keep working; money rules are still enforced by PostgreSQL. Set `fail-open: false` to reject with `503` instead, if abuse protection matters more than availability.
+- Redis timeouts are 500 ms, because the limiter call happens while the idempotency row lock is held.
+- Redis is excluded from the Kubernetes readiness probe, so a Redis outage doesn't take every API pod out of the Service.
+
+## Consequences
+- Fixed windows allow up to 2x the limit across a window boundary. Acceptable for a guard rail; a sliding window or token bucket is the upgrade path.
+- Redis runs without persistence: a restart resets counters and empties the cache, which is harmless.
diff --git a/docs/adr/0005-risk-service-in-java.md b/docs/adr/0005-risk-service-in-java.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..400b240
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/adr/0005-risk-service-in-java.md
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+# ADR 0005: A separate risk service, in Java, fed only by Kafka
+
+**Status:** accepted
+
+## Context
+Banks must watch for suspicious activity (AML): unusually large transfers, accounts sending a lot in one day, sudden bursts of transfers. Putting these checks inside the transfer transaction would slow every transfer and couple two very different concerns: moving money correctly vs. analysing behaviour.
+
+## Decision
+- A new service, `risk-service`, written in **Java 21 / Spring Boot**, consumes `transfer-events` in its **own consumer group** (`kirim-risk`). It receives every event independently of the notification consumer.
+- **Database per service:** it has its own database (`kirim_risk`) and never reads the transfer service's tables. Its only input is the event contract.
+- Rules are a pure function (`RiskRules`) with unit tests: `LARGE_TRANSFER`, `DAILY_TOTAL_EXCEEDED`, `HIGH_DAILY_COUNT`. Daily rules fire **once**, on the transfer that crosses the threshold, so one busy account doesn't flood the alert queue.
+- Days are bucketed in `Asia/Jakarta`, not UTC: a transfer at 01:00 WIB belongs to that WIB day.
+- Daily totals are updated with one atomic upsert (`INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE ... RETURNING`), so concurrent consumers can't lose an increment.
+- Same reliability pattern as the notification consumer: dedupe on `eventId` in the same transaction, retries with backoff, then its **own** dead-letter topic `transfer-events-risk-dlt`.
+- The event DTO uses strings for `type`/`status`, and unknown fields are ignored, so the producer can evolve without breaking this consumer.
+
+## Consequences
+- Alerts are **after the fact** (milliseconds to seconds later); this service detects, it does not block. Blocking a transfer in real time would need a synchronous check in the transfer path, with its own latency and availability trade-offs.
+- Two languages in one repo. That is deliberate here (Kotlin and Java both run on the JVM and use the same Spring stack) and mirrors teams where services are owned by different groups.
diff --git a/docs/adr/0006-kubernetes-and-gitlab-ci.md b/docs/adr/0006-kubernetes-and-gitlab-ci.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..c22ca5e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/adr/0006-kubernetes-and-gitlab-ci.md
@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+# ADR 0006: Kubernetes manifests with Kustomize, delivered by GitLab CI
+
+**Status:** accepted
+
+## Decision
+- Plain Kubernetes YAML in `deploy/k8s/base`, with **Kustomize overlays** (`local` for kind, `gitlab` for the CI deploy). No Helm: there is one app and two environments, and plain manifests are easier to review.
+- The API runs **2 replicas**, which the design already allows (outbox `SKIP LOCKED`, Redis-shared rate limits, Kafka consumer groups). A `PodDisruptionBudget` keeps one pod during node drains; rolling updates use `maxUnavailable: 0`.
+- Probes use Spring Boot's liveness/readiness groups. A **startup probe** covers JVM start and Flyway migrations, so slow starts don't trigger restarts.
+- Containers run as non-root with a read-only root filesystem (`/tmp` is an `emptyDir`) and no Linux capabilities.
+- Secrets are generated by Kustomize from **development-only** literals. A real cluster would use a secret manager (External Secrets, Sealed Secrets, Vault).
+- PostgreSQL, Kafka and Redis run in-cluster as single instances for development. Production would use managed services or operators (CloudNativePG, Strimzi) with replication.
+
+## GitLab CI
+- `test`: both Gradle builds with Testcontainers on Docker-in-Docker, the web build, and a manifest render check.
+- `build` (default branch): Docker images tagged with the **commit SHA**, pushed to the GitLab Container Registry.
+- `deploy` (manual): via the GitLab agent for Kubernetes. The job pins the overlay to this commit's images, applies it, and waits for `rollout status`, so a crash-looping release fails the pipeline.
+
+## Consequences
+- Rollback = re-run the deploy job of an earlier pipeline (images are immutable SHA tags).
+- GitHub Actions stays as the CI for the GitHub mirror; both pipelines run the same Gradle and npm commands.
